      "text": "From the definitions, we know that $\\sin(x+y)=\\sin(x)\\cos(y)+\\cos(x)\\sin(y)$. We want to determine all $y \\in [0,\\pi]$ for which\n\\[\\sin(x+y)\\leq \\sin(x)+\\sin(y)\\]\nfor every $x$ between $0$ and $\\pi$, inclusive.\n\nBy setting $x=0$ (i.e. $x=y$), we get that\n\\[\\sin(y+y)\\leq \\sin(0)+\\sin(y)\\]\nwhich is equivalent to\n\\[\\sin(2y)\\leq \\sin(y)\\]\n\nSince $F_G(y)=\\{\\sin(2y)\\leq \\sin(y)\\}$, we know that $F_A(y)=\\{\\sin(y) \\leq y\\}$ since $y\\leq 2y$.\n\nThis is a period-like function, as I know this coincides with $y$ on the interval $[0,\\pi/2]$ and ultimately coincides $2\\pi$ on the interval $[2\\pi,4\\pi]$ after considering $y \\in[0,\\pi]$.\n\nThe graph will be like all of this : $\\begin{begin}  \\begin{array} \\rfloor y  \\pm \\epsilon  _r \\mapsto \\save [laststyle]\\va \\save [color=\\bggreen] g \\gn \\dr \\ced \\save [color=\\bggreen]d  \\end{array} \\end{begin}$\n\n(Note: I need more/easier ships)\n\nYes, the coloring on the graph is incredible.\n\nWelcome to TopSage! ;)\n\nNo worries\nThank you! :D\n\nComing Soon!!!\n\nWell, apparently, $\\gcd\\{60,65,69,45\\}+60=210$. Draw a pythagorean triple with legs 69 and 16, and let me know if you're there.\nI am there\n\nWhat is the process to prove $x^6+x^5+x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ can be factored further as the product of two polynomials?\nWe use \"[x^2]y\".\nSee also the discussion http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=3780695#p3780695",
