      "text": "First, observe that the inequality \\(\\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\\) is valid for any \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in \\([0, \\pi]\\). Thus, no values of \\(y\\) can be excluded from the interval \\([0, \\pi]\\).\n\nWe seek \\(y \\in [0, \\pi]\\) for which \\(\\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\\) holds for all \\(x\\) in the same interval. We simplify this inequality using trigonometric identities:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x+y) = \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y).\n\\]\n\nSubstitute into the inequality:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin(x+y) &\\le \\sin(x) + \\sin(y) \\\\\n\\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) &\\le \\sin(x) + \\sin(y).\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we rearrange the inequality to isolate terms involving \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sin(x)\\cos(y) &\\le \\sin(x), \\\\\n\\cos(x)\\sin(y) &\\le \\sin(y).\n\\end{align*}\n\nFor \\(x \\in [0, \\pi]\\), we note that \\(\\sin(x) \\ge 0\\) and \\(\\cos(x) \\ge 0\\) on \\([0, \\pi]\\). \n\nConsider the cases where \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = \\pi\\):\n\n1) For \\(x = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\sin(0)\\cos(y) \\leq \\sin(0) + \\sin(y) \\implies 0 \\le \\sin(y),\n\\]\nwhich holds in \\([0, \\pi]\\).\n\n2) For \\(x = \\pi\\):\n\\[\n\\sin(\\pi)\\cos(y) \\leq \\sin(\\pi) + \\sin(y) \\implies 0 \\le \\sin(y),\n\\]\nwhich again holds within \\([0, \\pi]\\).\n\nBy examining other values of \\(x\\), consider \\(x \\neq 0\\) and \\(x \\neq \\pi\\). The equality \\(0 \\le Kx\\) must hold, where \\(K\\) is \\(\\cos(y)\\) or \\(\\sin(y)\\). Since \\(\\cos(y) \\ge 0\\) and \\(\\sin(y) \\ge 0\\), and \\(\\cos(y) \\le 1\\) and \\(\\sin(y) \\le 1\\), the only way to ensure our inequality \\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x)+\\sin(y)\\) holds for all \\(x\\) in \\([0, \\pi]\\) is to verify that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x+y) \\le \\sin(x) + \\sin(y) \\quad \\forall x \\in [0, \\pi].\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(y \\in [\\pi, 2\\pi]\\), this condition clearly fails as the sine function oscillates between positive and negative values resulting in the inequality not holding across the entire domain.\n\nThus the only interval for \\(y\\) that retains this inequality is when \\(y \\in (0, \\pi]\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(0, \\pi\\right]}\n\\]",
