      "text": "Let's consider \\( f(x, y) = \\sin(x+y) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(y) \\). To find the values of \\( y \\) for which \\( f(x, y) \\leq 0 \\) for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\), we need to look at the function \\( f(x, y) \\) for different intervals of \\( x \\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( 0 \\leq x < \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\)\nIn this range, \\( \\sin(x) \\geq 0 \\). Given the continuity of the sine function, we know that:\n- \\( \\sin(x) > 0 \\)\n  The function \\( f(x, y) = \\sin(x+y) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(y) \\) has a maximum value when \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n  \\[\n  f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, y\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + y\\right) - \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) - \\sin(y) = 1 - 1 - \\sin(y) = -\\sin(y) \\leq 0 \\quad \\text{if } y = 0.\n  \\]\n  Therefore, \\( y = 0 \\) works in this interval.\n\n### Case 2: \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\leq x < \\pi \\)\nIn this range, \\( \\sin(x) \\leq 1 \\). Given the continuity of the sine function, we know that:\n- \\( \\sin(x) \\leq 1 \\)\n  The function \\( f(x, y) = \\sin(x+y) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(y) \\) simplifies to:\n  \\[\n  f\\left(x, y\\right) = \\sin(x+y) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(y).\n  \\]\n  To find the specific values of \\( y \\), we analyze the behavior of the sine function at key points:\n  - At \\( y = 0 \\), \\( f(x, 0) = \\sin(x) - \\sin(x) = 0 \\), which is true for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\).\n  - At \\( y = \\pi \\), \\( f(x, \\pi) = \\sin(x+\\pi) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(\\pi) = -\\sin(x) + \\sin(x) = 0 \\), which is true for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\).\n\n### Summary of Solutions\nThe solutions provided by solving the inequalities are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 0, \\pi}\n\\]",
